  "subtype": "causal_attribution",
  "outcome_variable": "audience_dissatisfaction",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "writing_quality_issue",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Categorizes a candidate cause (poor dialogue, uninspired/cliched writing, incoherent narrative) whose effect on dissatisfaction is being attributed. This is a plausible driver of the outcome, i.e., a treatment/cause, not a confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "plot_structural_issue",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Another candidate cause of dissatisfaction (plot holes, no plot, underdeveloped characters). Like writing quality, it is a proposed cause, not a common cause of another treatment and the outcome."
    },
    {
      "name": "genre_execution_quality",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Genre (failed drama/horror/comedy/etc.) plausibly affects both the type of writing/plot issues that arise (treatment) and audience expectations/dissatisfaction (outcome), so it could act as a common cause. However, its 'failed_X' framing entangles it with the outcome, weakening its status as a clean confounder."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["writing_quality_issue", "plot_structural_issue"],
  "confounders": [
    {"name": "genre_execution_quality", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.3}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.2,
  "rationale": "The added columns supply candidate causes (treatments): writing_quality_issue and plot_structural_issue, both surfaced from the review text rather than the query. However, the operator fails the decisive causal-attribution test: it provides essentially NO genuine confounders -- variables that are common causes of both a treatment and the outcome and must be adjusted to avoid bias. The only candidate, genre_execution_quality, is itself framed as a failure/quality outcome ('failed_drama') and thus behaves more like another treatment or an outcome-entangled variable than a clean pre-treatment common cause; pure genre (independent of execution) would be a better confounder but was not isolated. No demographic, exposure, rating-baseline, or reviewer-level common causes are surfaced. The set therefore identifies plausible causes but does not set up an unbiased causal estimation, so confounder quality is low."
}
